---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Is Meridian PSI Worth the Price?',
  description:
    'Meridian Core (the live behavioral monitor plus Intel analytics and a journal) is free, no card. Meridian Guard, the enforcement layer, is $29.99/mo, cancel anytime. So the price question is a Guard question: against a one-time hard-limit add-on it costs more over time, because it enforces on behavior as well as the dollar line. Priced against one revenge session ($300–800) or a lost eval ($89–540), the math changes.',
  robots: { index: true, follow: true },
};

const question = 'Is Meridian PSI worth the price?';
const directAnswer =
  'Meridian Core is free, with no card and no trial clock: the live behavioral monitor (seven signals scored into a real-time PSI against your own baseline) and the Intel/Stats analytics workspace with a built-in journal — the piece traders otherwise pay a separate journal subscription for. Meridian Guard, the enforcement layer (every traditional limit, enforced at the order layer, plus Strict Lock), is $29.99/mo, cancel anytime. So the price question is really a Guard question. Against a one-time $99 hard-limit add-on, Guard costs more over time — because it is not the same product: it enforces on the behavior as well as the dollar line. Priced against the problem instead of the category, the math is short: a single revenge session commonly runs $300–800 before a daily loss limit even fires, and a futures eval costs $89–540 and is gone the day you break its rules. One such session costs more than ten months of Guard. Meridian does not promise to catch every one and it will not make you profitable — but Core costs nothing to run, and on NinjaTrader 8 your first 7 days on a free license include Guard, no card, so your own Intel data, not the sticker price, makes the call.';

const qaSchema = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'QAPage',
  datePublished: '2026-07-04',
  dateModified: '2026-09-23',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    text: question,
    answerCount: 1,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: directAnswer,
      url: 'https://www.meridianpsi.com/answers/is-meridian-psi-worth-the-price',
    },
  },
}).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={qaSchema} />

  <article class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] uppercase mb-3" style="color:var(--aw-color-primary);">
        Answer · Pricing &amp; value
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-zinc-900 leading-tight tracking-tight mb-6">
        {question}
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-6">Last reviewed: September 2026</p>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200 px-6 py-5" style="background:#f0fdf4;">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.12em] uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--aw-color-primary);">
          Short answer
        </p>
        <p class="text-[15px] text-zinc-700 leading-relaxed">{directAnswer}</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div
      class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-p:text-[17px] prose-p:leading-[1.85] prose-headings:font-bold prose-headings:text-zinc-900 prose-h2:text-[1.35rem] prose-h2:mt-10 prose-h2:mb-3"
    >
      <h2>What is free, and what Guard adds</h2>
      <p>
        Three layers, one product, one license across every supported platform. First, the live monitor (free in Core):
        seven behavioral signals — Revenge Entry, Size Spike, Stop Manipulation, Overtrading Pace, Hold Bias, Position
        Overstay, Rule Violations — scored against your own adaptive baseline into a real-time
        <strong>PSI (Psychological Stability Index)</strong>, updated within 100ms of every fill. Second, enforcement
        (the Guard tier, $29.99/mo): six triggers across five response levels, from a quiet notify up to a Trading Pause
        or broker disconnect, with Strict Lock so the tilted version of you cannot switch it off mid-session — plus
        every traditional limit (daily loss, loss-streak, single-trade, drawdown, session time, Hard limits) enforced at
        the order layer. Third, the review layer (<strong>Intel and Stats, free in Core</strong>): your discipline in
        actual numbers, session by session, with a built-in journal and five years of local history.
      </p>

      <h2>Compared to what?</h2>
      <p>
        Most serious traders already pay a monthly subscription for a trading journal that covers a slice of the third
        layer — after the fact. The one-time hard-limit add-ons cover a slice of the second layer, at the line. Nothing
        else in the stack does the first layer at all. So the honest comparison is not &ldquo;Meridian vs. a $99
        one-time tool&rdquo; — it is Meridian vs. the stack you would assemble to replace it: a journal subscription,
        plus a risk add-on, plus a behavioral layer you cannot buy separately, because no one else ships one. Priced
        that way, the monitor and the review layer cost nothing: Core is free, and the only line item left is Guard at
        $29.99 a month.
      </p>

      <h2>The math against the problem</h2>
      <p>
        Price the tool against the thing it is aimed at. A futures eval costs $89&ndash;540 and is gone the day you
        break its rules. A single revenge session commonly runs $300&ndash;800 before a daily loss limit even speaks.
        One of those sessions costs more than ten months of Guard; the eval it takes down costs more still. Set against
        that ledger, Guard is $29.99 a month, and the monitor that spots the session going wrong is free.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our own published session data (230+ round trips across 32 live sessions, July 2026) puts numbers on that gap:
        when P&amp;L kept falling after the first Warning-zone flag, the median run to the trough was 2 more trades,
        about 14 minutes, and <strong>$392 further down</strong> — one median post-flag spiral costs more than a year of
        Guard. The worst recorded run was $2,571, in a session where the trader&rsquo;s own $300 daily-loss line was breached
        more than 7× in under a minute. The full data set:
        <a href="/answers/does-behavioral-risk-management-actually-work" class="text-emerald-700 underline"
          >does behavioral risk management actually work</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>Why a $99 hard-limit tool doesn&rsquo;t make Meridian redundant</h2>
      <p>
        Keep the hard limits — Meridian Guard ships them too, enforced at the order layer. The part a fixed rule cannot
        express is the behavioral read, and that part is free in Core. A $500 daily stop lets four revenge trades
        through before it fires; the Revenge Entry signal flags the second one, while there is still session left to
        save. And the patterns that actually drain accounts rarely fit an if/then rule: size that creeps up gradually
        instead of doubling, re-entries that wait ninety seconds instead of ten, two losses that tilt you on a Tuesday
        and roll off you on a Friday. A fixed rule needs you to predict your failure in advance and write it as a
        number. A baseline model just notices you have stopped trading like yourself — and acts before the number is
        hit. The full comparison:
        <a href="/answers/meridian-vs-daily-loss-limit" class="text-emerald-700 underline"
          >Meridian vs. daily loss limit</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>The part no journal shows you</h2>
      <p>
        Intel splits every session on two axes: how disciplined you were (PSI) and what you made (P&amp;L). A lucky
        green day gets labeled what it is. A disciplined red day stops reading as failure. Over weeks it puts a dollar
        figure on what your unstable stretches cost against your stable ones — broken down by time of day, instrument,
        and signal. A P&amp;L journal cannot build this view, because it has no live behavioral engine to know which
        trades you took while tilting. That is why Intel ships in free Core: it is the improvement loop, and Guard adds
        the brake. More on the workspace:
        <a href="/history-intel" class="text-emerald-700 underline">History &amp; Intel</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What neither tier buys</h2>
      <p>
        It will not make you profitable, and it does not claim to. PSI is a behavioral proxy built from your trading
        activity — not physiology, and not a profit predictor. It cannot promise a green day, and it does not replace an
        edge. What Meridian gives you is the layer between &ldquo;trading normally&rdquo; and &ldquo;hitting your
        number,&rdquo; plus the numbers to judge whether that layer is earning its keep for you. That judgment is what
        <a href="/pricing" class="text-emerald-700 underline">free Core</a> is for. On NinjaTrader 8, your first 7 days on
        a free license include Guard, no card.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50/80 px-5 py-4">
      <svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-emerald-600" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20"
        ><path
          fill-rule="evenodd"
          d="M10 18a8 8 0 100-16 8 8 0 000 16zm3.857-9.809a.75.75 0 00-1.214-.882l-3.483 4.79-1.88-1.88a.75.75 0 10-1.06 1.061l2.5 2.5a.75.75 0 001.137-.089l4-5.5z"
          clip-rule="evenodd"></path></svg
      >
      <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-zinc-800">Official NinjaTrader Ecosystem Vendor.</strong> Meridian is an approved NinjaTrader
        Ecosystem vendor; it is not owned by or affiliated with NinjaTrader, Tradovate, or Ironbeam. Trading involves substantial
        risk of loss. Meridian does not provide trading signals or investment advice. Results may vary.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 pt-10 border-t border-zinc-100">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="https://whop.com/checkout/plan_ywOyNPvDxrxxV"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-sm font-semibold text-white"
          style="background:var(--aw-color-primary);"
        >
          Get Meridian free
        </a>
        <a
          href="/answers"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-sm font-semibold border border-zinc-300 text-zinc-700 hover:border-zinc-500 transition-colors"
        >
          More answers →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
